fact WorkItem(id: "WI-552", description: some(value: "Canonicalize binder/parameter references to var_ref(name); retire the WI-067 discharge normalize pass. SURFACED BY WI-067 (effect discharge): a reference to a LOCAL binder / operation parameter has TWO representations that do not unify — var_ref(name) (the canonical form, minted by binder_ref_value for Γ facts + occurrence_to_term / Expr::VarRef, settled by WI-550) vs bare Ref(sym) (minted by the effect-rekey substitute_ref_syms, the Modify[c] -> Modify[s] rename, which never got the var_ref memo). So a guard eq(b,0), after the rekey rewrites b to the caller binder, is eq(Ref(caller_b),0) while Γ holds neq(var_ref(caller_b),0) — they do not unify. WI-067 papers over this with TWO consumer-side patches: (1) normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref (rewrite the guard's Ref -> var_ref at discharge time) and (2) the resolver's value_has_open_world_ref recognizing var_ref-ONLY as the open-world binder (a bare Ref must stay decidable so a reflective builtin a proof chases — scope/is_entity_of — still resolves). Three sync points for one concept; binder_ref_value's own doc-contract ('if the canonical binder form ever moves, both producers move with it') is ALREADY VIOLATED by the rekey. ROOT CAUSE: bare Ref(sym) is OVERLOADED — it means both 'a global (sort/op/const/constructor — closed, decide)' and 'a local binder (open-world, flounder)'; var_ref is precisely the disambiguator and one producer just does not emit it. REFACTOR: make var_ref(name) the SINGLE canonical form for a local binder/param everywhere, Ref(sym) reserved for globals. Concretely substitute_ref_syms emits var_ref(arg) (not Ref(arg)) when it rewrites a param to a binder argument — and its targets ARE binder refs (it is fed by extract_var_ref_sym_node), so this is semantically exact, not a hack. Then guards carry var_ref natively, unify with Γ with NO normalize pass, and the resolver's open-world rule becomes a principled 'var_ref => open-world' instead of 'gated on the gamma overlay + relies on a pre-normalize having run'. CATCH (blast radius — why deferred not done): the Modify[.] resource readers — effect-region masking (WI-351/353 keep/flow), eval, cpp-gen, the effect-row printer/canonicalizer — currently read the resource as a bare Ref(s); if the rekey emits var_ref(s) for LABELS too (a blind whole-value rekey cannot tell guard-position from label-position) each must learn var_ref. Smaller alternative: var_ref-ify ONLY the guard sub-term at the producer (teach substitute_ref_syms guard-vs-label position-awareness) — net-even with the current normalize-at-consumer, so the real win is the full label+guard canonicalization. BENEFIT: retire normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref; collapse three sync points to one (KnowledgeBase::make_var_ref_term + binder_ref_value); the resolver's open-world floundering becomes principled instead of gamma-gated-plus-pre-normalized; restores binder_ref_value's one-canonical-form contract. NON-BLOCKING: the current WI-067 fix is sound and gamma-scoped so it cannot leak into non-discharge resolution — this is altitude/maintainability, not a correctness fix. TRIGGER: WI-539 (call-site requires/ensures over Γ) is the same-shaped SECOND consumer (its preconditions are the same guard shape) — doing this before/with WI-539 avoids adding a fourth patch. Acceptance: cargo-test green; normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref removed and the resolver open-world recognition principled (var_ref = open-world, no gamma-pre-normalize dependency), WI-067's discharge tests still green."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-06-24T10:30:30Z"))

fact Tag(workitem: "WI-552", name: "p048")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-552", author: "claude", content: "DELIVERED 2026-06-24 (commit 085b6c9 + merge with origin WI-027; full workspace suite green 2282). Approach A (chosen by user after a var_ref-vs-Ref design discussion): a binder/parameter occurrence IS a VARIABLE, so it is canonicalized to var_ref(name) at the PRODUCER, with bare Ref reserved for closed globals. The bare Ref(b) the effect-rekey emitted was the mis-representation that forced the WI-067 discharge-time normalize patch.\n\nWHAT (load.rs + typing.rs): (1) PRODUCER -- requires/ensures clauses (convert_clause_list) and guarded-effect guards (EffectGuarded arm) store their op-frame VARIABLE occurrences as var_ref at LOAD via wrap_places_as_var_ref. Variable set = this signature's params+result (exact symbols, signature_place_types) PLUS callback parameter/result places (unambiguous CallbackParam/CallbackResult kinds). Globals (sort/op/const/constructor) stay decidable bare Ref -- strictly MORE precise than the old normalize's \"any non-constructor ref\" net, which over-wrapped globals. (2) sigma -- substitute_ref_terms is now a proper VARIABLE substitution: replaces a whole var_ref(name:Ref(s)) node when s is in the sigma domain (never recurses into the wrapper -> no var_ref(name:0) corruption); a malformed name child trips a debug_assert (loud-over-silent). (3) Deleted normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref + normalize_param_refs_rec + their 3 discharge call sites. Guards/preconditions carry var_ref natively, unify with Gamma, flounder as the open-world parameter -- no consumer-side normalize.\n\nACCEPTANCE MET LITERALLY: normalize_param_refs_to_var_ref removed; resolver value_has_open_world_ref recognition is principled (var_ref => open-world, no pre-discharge normalize dependency -- doc restored); WI-067 discharge + WI-539 contract + WI-347 refinement tests green.\n\nLABELS STAY BARE Ref: the ticket's \"full label+guard canonicalization\" framing was a misread -- a Modify[c] resource label is a SortRef read by extract_effect_resource_sym, NOT a binder; var_ref-ifying it would break region masking. Only GUARD/clause goal refs are var_ref'd. The call-site effect rekey (substitute_ref_syms) preserves var_ref automatically by renaming the inner symbol of var_ref(name:Ref(c))->var_ref(name:Ref(s)); WI-347 substitute_clause compares var_ref clauses like-to-like.\n\n/code-review (5 finders, high effort): NO correctness bugs. Caught + fixed before delivery: (a) callback-place COMPLETENESS gap -- the old normalize wrapped callback places too; extended the wrap predicate to CallbackParam/CallbackResult kinds (latent, no current trigger, but closes the removed safety net); (b) loud None arm (debug_assert) on a malformed var_ref reaching sigma. Confirmed sound: WI-347 like-to-like, the sigma layer (reflect-term substitution, rightly distinct from the resolver logic-var machinery -- folding would violate the WI-348 boundary), term_references_any finds a surviving param inside its var_ref wrapper. Altitude confirmed right depth: op-BODY binders already become var_ref via occurrence_to_term's VarRef arm (WI-537), so wrapping only the two clause/guard contexts that flowed through bare convert_term is principled, not a bandaid.\n\nKEY CONCEPTUAL FRAMING (user-clarified): var_ref = a VARIABLE identified by name (the reflect Expr.var_ref constructor); bare Ref(sym) = a reference to a closed named entity (a global). The open-world/flounder behavior is a CONSEQUENCE of being a variable (NAF/closed-world reasoning is invalid over a runtime-unknown), NOT its definition.\n\nMerged cleanly with origin WI-027 (bounded quantification); both touched resolve.rs (auto-merged, different regions).", at: "2026-06-24T10:31:06Z")
